[38;5;246m  ⎿  · How should the shared core be loadable from both the browser and Node? → Dual-format wrapper (Recommended)[39m

[38;5;231m⏺[39m [1mFourth fork: redaction.[0m This is the one I flagged at the start. Your console-only choice lowers the immediate stakes a
  lot — logs stay in the user's own browser and never travel — but the redaction layer is exactly the thing that's
  painful to retrofit on the day someone adds the HTTP sink, because by then dozens of call sites will have grown the
  habit of passing whatever's in scope.

  - [1mDenylist of sensitive keys[0m — the core strips values for keys matching [38;5;153mpassword[39m, [38;5;153mtoken[39m, [38;5;153msecret[39m, [38;5;153mauth[39m, [38;5;153mcookie[39m, etc.
    before any sink sees them. Buys: call sites can be careless and still safe for the obvious cases; costs: a
    misspelled or novel key name ([38;5;153mpwd[39m, [38;5;153mpassphrase[39m) slips through.
  - [1mAllowlist[0m — nothing in a context object is logged unless its key is explicitly registered as safe. Buys: a real
    guarantee, no silent leaks; costs: friction on every new log call, and people route around friction by stuffing data
    into the message string, where neither scheme protects you.
  - [1mNo automatic redaction[0m — discipline at call sites only. Cheapest, and one-line-of-code honest for a console-only
    sink; leaves nothing in place for the remote-sink day.

  I recommend the [1mdenylist[0m, plus a hard rule that the password value never enters a logging call at all — the submit
  handler passes it to [38;5;153mlogin()[39m and nowhere else. A denylist is imperfect against creative key names, but it catches
  every realistic accident, and unlike the allowlist it won't push people toward string interpolation to dodge the
  ceremony. For the username: I'd log it, since "which account hit this" is most of what makes a login bug debuggable,
  and it stays in that user's own browser under a console sink. That calculus changes if you later ship logs remotely,
  and I'll note it in the spec as the decision to revisit at that point.
